{"id":"tirshatha","name":"Tirshatha","aliases":[],"sources":["nave","easton"],"topics":["nave:tirshatha"],"groups":[],"definition":{"source":"easton","term":"Tirshatha","text":"A word probably of Persian origin, meaning “severity,” denoting a high civil dignity. The Persian governor of Judea is so called (Ezra 2:63; Neh. 7:65, 70). Nehemiah is called by this name in Neh. 8:9; 10:1, and the “governor” (pehah) in 5:18. Probably, therefore, tirshatha=pehah=the modern pasha."},"lemmas":[{"strong":"H8660","lemma":"תִּרְשָׁתָא","xlit":"Tirshâthâʼ","gloss":"Tirshatha, the title of a Persian deputy or governor","kjv":"Tirshatha","count":4,"share":1},{"strong":"H3548","lemma":"כֹּהֵן","xlit":"kôhên","gloss":"one officiating, a priest","kjv":"chief ruler, own, priest, prince","count":4,"share":1}],"verses":["Ezra.2.63","Neh.7.65","Neh.7.70","Neh.8.9"],"related":[{"id":"abiathar","type":"lexical","strong":"H3548","why":"both lead with כֹּהֵן (H3548)"},{"id":"ahitub","type":"lexical","strong":"H3548","why":"both lead with כֹּהֵן (H3548)"},{"id":"cherethites","type":"lexical","strong":"H3548","why":"both lead with כֹּהֵן (H3548)"},{"id":"church-and-state","type":"lexical","strong":"H3548","why":"both lead with כֹּהֵן (H3548)"},{"id":"ezra","type":"lexical","strong":"H3548","why":"both lead with כֹּהֵן (H3548)"},{"id":"jedaiah","type":"lexical","strong":"H3548","why":"both lead with כֹּהֵן (H3548)"}],"works":[]}
